{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with smaller average fish body size (biomass per individual) exhibit higher production‐to‐biomass (P/B) ratios, controlling for SST and biomass density.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether mean individual fish size is negatively related to turnover (P/B) rates, indicating that communities dominated by smaller fish have faster biomass turnover.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute mean individual size for each transect: size = biomass (kg or t/ha) divided by abundance (fish counts).\n2. Calculate P/B ratio for each transect: prod/biomass, filtering out zero‐biomass or zero‐abundance cases.\n3. Aggregate to reef‐year‐depth level to obtain mean size, mean P/B, mean_sst, and mean biomass density.\n4. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model with mean P/B as response; fixed effects: mean size, mean_sst, mean biomass; random intercepts for id_reef (or region).\n5. Check the significance and sign of the mean‐size coefficient; validate model assumptions (residual plots, Q–Q).\n6. Optionally include an interaction between mean size and mean_sst to see if the size–P/B relationship changes with temperature variability.","deliverables":"• Table of mixed‐model coefficients (especially mean size effect) with confidence intervals.\n• Diagnostic plots of residuals vs fitted and normal Q–Q.\n• Scatterplot of mean P/B vs mean size colored by mean_sst with fitted regression plane or lines."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The strength of the negative biomass–turnover relationship has weakened over time (i.e., the community’s compensatory response has changed across decades).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether the slope of turnover ~ biomass changes between early and late study periods, indicating temporal shifts in community dynamics.",  
      "steps":"1. Define a period factor: e.g., Pre‐2010 vs Post‐2010 (or split at median year).\n2. Compute turnover and biomass at transect level (or aggregate to reef‐year‐depth).\n3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: turnover ~ biomass * period + (1|id_reef).\n4. Examine the biomass × period interaction coefficient and its significance to test for slope change.\n5. Perform model diagnostics and ensure no violation of homoscedasticity or normality.\n6. Visualize turnover vs biomass scatterplots with separate regression lines for each period.","deliverables":"• Mixed‐model output showing biomass × period interaction estimate and p‐value.\n• Diagnostic plots (residuals vs fitted, Q–Q).\n• Overlayed scatterplots of turnover vs biomass with period‐specific fitted lines."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with greater long‐term SST variability show higher interannual variability in their P/B ratios, indicating that thermal fluctuations drive instability in turnover.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether sites experiencing more variable thermal environments have correspondingly more variable biomass turnover between years.",  
      "steps":"1. For each id_reef, calculate long‐term SST variability: standard deviation of mean_sst across all years.\n2. Compute P/B ratio per reef‐year (aggregated across depths): prod_sum/biomass_sum.\n3. For each id_reef calculate interannual P/B variability: standard deviation of reef‐year P/B over time.\n4. Fit a linear regression or mixed model: P/B variability ~ SST variability + (1|region).\n5. Test significance of SST variability coefficient; check collinearity.\n6. Plot P/B variability vs SST variability with regression line.","deliverables":"• Regression table with coefficient for SST variability and confidence intervals.\n• Scatterplot of P/B variability vs SST variability colored by region.\n• Diagnostic plots for regression assumptions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs at the northernmost and southernmost latitudes exhibit lower interannual P/B variability than mid‐Gulf reefs, reflecting more stable communities at biogeographic extremes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether latitude extremes are associated with reduced turnover variability relative to mid‐latitude reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute mean latitude (degree) per reef.\n2. Classify reefs into three bins: Lower 20% (south extreme), Middle 60% (mid‐Gulf), Upper 20% (north extreme).\n3. Compute interannual P/B variability per reef as above.\n4. Compare P/B variability across latitude bins using ANOVA or a mixed model: variability ~ latitude_bin + (1|region).\n5. Conduct post‐hoc pairwise tests (Tukey) to see if extremes differ from mid‐Gulf.\n6. Check residuals for ANOVA or model diagnostics.","deliverables":"• ANOVA table or mixed‐model summary with latitude bin effects.\n• Boxplots of P/B variability by latitude bin with significance annotations.\n• Post‐hoc test results."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"At the reef‐year scale, the absolute difference in P/B between shallow (5 m) and deep (20 m) strata decreases with increasing total reef biomass, implying that high‐biomass reefs have more homogenized turnover across depths.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether differences in depth‐specific turnover narrow as total biomass increases.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, aggregate transect data separately for depth = 5 m and 20 m to get mean P/B_shallow and P/B_deep.\n2. Compute absolute difference ΔP/B = |P/B_deep – P/B_shallow|.\n3. Also aggregate total biomass per reef‐year (sum or mean across depths).\n4. Fit a regression or mixed model: ΔP/B ~ total biomass + (1|region).\n5. Check coefficient for total biomass (expect negative).\n6. Validate assumptions and visualize ΔP/B vs biomass with fitted line.","deliverables":"• Model output with biomass effect on ΔP/B and CIs.\n• Scatterplot of ΔP/B vs total biomass with regression line.\n• Residual diagnostics plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Positive within‐year SST anomalies (relative to long‐term reef mean) are associated with elevated P/B ratios, indicating a compensatory productivity response to thermal stress.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether “hot” years yield higher turnover per biomass relative to the reef’s background temperature.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, compute its long‐term mean SST across all years.\n2. For each reef‐year, calculate SST anomaly = mean_sst_year – long‐term_mean_sst.\n3. Compute reef‐year P/B ratio as prod_sum/biomass_sum.\n4. Fit a mixed model: P/B ~ SST_anomaly + mean biomass + (1|id_reef).\n5. Assess the sign and significance of SST_anomaly coefficient.\n6. Run diagnostics and check for nonlinearity (e.g., include quadratic anomaly term if needed).\n7. Plot P/B vs SST_anomaly with a fitted line.","deliverables":"• Mixed‐model summary for SST anomaly effect.\n• Plot of P/B ratio vs SST anomaly with regression fit.\n• Residual vs fitted and Q–Q diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Across the entire study period, mean P/B ratios have declined, after accounting for region, reflecting a long‐term slowdown in biomass turnover across the Gulf.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Detect a temporal trend in productivity‐to‐biomass ratios at the regional scale.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute reef‐year P/B ratios per reef as prod_sum/biomass_sum.\n2. Fit a linear mixed model: P/B ~ year (continuous) + (1|region).\n3. Examine the year coefficient; test if negative and significant.\n4. Check for nonlinear trend by comparing with a model including a year^2 term or a spline.\n5. Validate residual assumptions.\n6. Plot regional‐level fitted P/B trend lines over time.","deliverables":"• Model coefficients for year (and year^2, if used) with CIs.\n• Time series plot of observed vs fitted mean P/B per region.\n• Residual diagnostics."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The spatial variance of reef‐year P/B ratios within each region has declined over time, indicating homogenization of productivity dynamics among reefs in the same region.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether within‐region dispersion in productivity rates has contracted, signifying more similar P/B across reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. For each region and year, compute the variance (or standard deviation) of reef‐year P/B across all reefs in that region.\n2. Construct a time series of regional P/B variance.\n3. Fit a linear model or mixed model: P/B_variance ~ year + (1|region).\n4. Evaluate whether the slope for year is negative and significant.\n5. Check diagnostics for homoscedasticity and normality.\n6. Visualize regional P/B variance over time with regression lines.","deliverables":"• Model output for trend in P/B variance over years.\n• Plots of P/B variance vs year for each region with trend lines.\n• Diagnostic plots for model assumptions."  
    }  
  }  
]}
